# Icon System Rules (Lucide Icons)

The theme has one curated set of ~30 Lucide icons (see
`src/js-admin/lucide-icon-options.js` → `CHAIRFORCE_LUCIDE_ICON_OPTIONS` —
the single source of truth for slugs/labels) consumed by **three separate
surfaces**, each solving a different rendering constraint:

| Surface | How icons render | When to use |
|---|---|---|
| Button block picker | Icon font (`::before` CSS glyph) | Editing a `core/button` block. |
| PHP programmatic buttons | Icon font via `chairforce_get_buttons_markup()` | Dynamic buttons output from PHP — see [Helper Functions](08-helper-functions.mdc) § Programmatic Buttons. |
| Icon Block plugin | Inline SVG (plugin's own block) | Editors inserting a standalone icon anywhere in content. |
| Sass mixins | Icon font (`::before` CSS glyph) | Styling markup you don't control (plugins, WooCommerce templates) - no class injection needed. |

## Why This Exists

The block editor's canvas is rendered inside an `<iframe>` (WP 6.3+). Any CSS that
needs to visually render inside block **content** (not just editor UI) must be
loaded via `enqueue_block_assets`, never `enqueue_block_editor_assets` — the
latter only loads in the editor chrome, not inside the iframe, and WordPress
will warn `"...-style-css was added to the iframe incorrectly"` if you try.

Icon SVGs also can't be injected as raw `<svg data-lucide>` markup + a JS
`createIcons()` call for the Button block, because that JS never runs against
the iframed editor canvas reliably. **Button block icons render as CSS (icon
font), not JS-injected SVG.**

## Surface 1: Button Block Picker (Icon Font)

| Piece | Location | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Icon font CSS | `assets/css/button-icon-font.css` | Static, hand-authored `@font-face` + `content` rules. **Not part of the webpack/Sass build.** |
| Font files | `assets/fonts/lucide/lucide.woff(2)` | Self-hosted Lucide icon font (subset, no SVG fallback). |
| PHP enqueue | `lib/class-lucide-icons.php` (`Chairforce\Lucide_Icons`) | Enqueues `button-icon-font.css` via `enqueue_block_assets` so it loads in the editor iframe **and** on the frontend, globally. Registered in `lib/class-init.php`. |
| Curated icon list | `src/js-admin/lucide-icon-options.js` | `CHAIRFORCE_LUCIDE_ICON_OPTIONS` — the ~30 icon slugs matching Figma. Admin-only bundle. |
| Editor UI | `src/js-admin/button-icons.js` | Registers `chairforceIcon` / `chairforceIconPosition` attributes on `core/button`, adds a `ComboboxControl` + preview swatch in `InspectorControls`, and builds the `cf-*` class names applied to `save()`/frontend markup. |
| Picker styling | `src/sass-admin/_button-icon-picker.scss` | Editor-only sidebar styling for the combobox + preview swatch. |

### Why the Icon Font CSS Is NOT in the Webpack/Sass Pipeline

Do not `@import` `lucide-static`'s CSS/font into Sass or JS. Three build-pipeline
issues were hit and are why this is a static, manually-maintained file instead:

1. **Tree-shaking removes it**: `lucide-static`'s `package.json` sets
   `"sideEffects": false`, so webpack strips the CSS import in production builds.
2. **Font bloat**: The upstream `lucide.css` embeds a ~19MB SVG font fallback via
   `@font-face`, ballooning `build/index.css` to 25MB+ if bundled directly.
3. **Minifier corruption**: cssnano (via `postcss-loader`) was silently wiping
   `content: "\eXXX"` **escape-sequence** values down to empty strings during
   minification. (Literal glyph characters do survive the pipeline - see
   Surface 3 below, which relies on exactly that.)

**Rule: keep `assets/css/button-icon-font.css` as a static asset outside the
build pipeline.** It only contains a minimal `woff2`/`woff` `@font-face` and
`content: "\eXXX"` rules for the icons actually in the curated list.

### Class Naming Convention

All classes for this feature use a `cf-` prefix to avoid colliding with
generically-named classes from other plugins/themes (e.g. a prior `has-icon`
class collided with third-party plugin CSS):

- `cf-has-icon` — applied to `.wp-block-button` when an icon is set
- `cf-icon-left` / `cf-icon-right` — icon position
- `cf-icon-{slug}` — the specific icon glyph (e.g. `cf-icon-shopping-cart`)
- `cf-icon-preview` — generic preview swatch (editor picker)
- `cf-icon-picker`, `cf-icon-picker__combobox`, `cf-icon-picker__preview`,
  `cf-icon-picker__preview-empty`, `cf-icon-picker__option` — picker UI structure

No backward compatibility is required for these class names (new theme, no
legacy content).

## Surface 2: Icon Block Plugin Integration

**The Icon Block** (`outermost/icon-block`, by Nick Diego - must be installed
+ active) is a standalone block for inserting one-off icons anywhere in
content. It's unrelated to `core/button` and unrelated to WordPress core's
own native `core/icon` block (see Future section below). It exposes a
documented JS filter, `iconBlock.icons`, for third-party icon registration:
https://nickdiego.com/adding-custom-icons-to-the-icon-block

| Piece | Location | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Icon data | `src/js-admin/chairforce-icon-block-icons.js` | `CHAIRFORCE_ICON_BLOCK_ICONS` - same 30 slugs/labels as `lucide-icon-options.js`, reshaped for the plugin: `{ name, title, icon (raw SVG), categories: ['chairforce'], hasNoIconFill: true }`. SVG markup copied by hand from `node_modules/lucide-static/icons/{slug}.svg` with the root `<svg>`'s `width`/`height`/`class` attributes stripped (plugin controls sizing; child shapes left untouched). No generator script - fixed, small (~30) curated set, kept manually in sync with `lucide-icon-options.js`. |
| Filter registration | `src/js-admin/register-custom-icons.js` | Registers `iconBlock.icons` (namespace `chairforce/icon-block-custom-icons`), adding a `"ChairForce"` icon type/category alongside the plugin's own default icons. |
| Wiring | `src/js-admin/index.js` | `import './register-custom-icons';` - bundled into the same `build/index.js` already enqueued via `enqueue_block_editor_assets`. **No PHP changes needed.** |

### `hasNoIconFill: true` Is Mandatory

Lucide icons are **stroke-based** (`stroke="currentColor"`, `fill="none"`),
unlike the plugin's own default icon set, which is fill-based. The plugin's
`hasNoIconFill` flag exists specifically for this ("set to `true` for icons
that use `stroke` attributes and/or should not have a color fill applied").
Every entry in `chairforce-icon-block-icons.js` must set it, or the plugin's
color control will fight with the icon's `fill="none"` and can render
incorrectly.

### How to Add/Remove an Icon Here

1. Update `CHAIRFORCE_LUCIDE_ICON_OPTIONS` in `lucide-icon-options.js` first
   (source of truth).
2. Add/remove the matching entry in `chairforce-icon-block-icons.js`, copying
   the SVG body from `node_modules/lucide-static/icons/{slug}.svg` (strip
   `width`/`height`/`class` from the root `<svg>` tag only).
3. `npm run build:assets`.

## Surface 3: CSS-Only Icons via Sass Mixins

For markup we don't control - WooCommerce templates, WC Blocks' interactive
buttons, other plugin output - there's no way to inject a `cf-icon-{slug}`
class via JS/PHP. Instead, target the plugin's own existing selector directly
in Sass and pull in the icon via a mixin - no markup changes needed at all.

| Piece | Location | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Font family + codepoints | `src/sass/icon-font/_variables.scss` | `$cf-icon-font-family` and the `$cf-icon-codepoints` map (same ~30 slugs, same codepoints as `button-icon-font.css`). |
| Mixins | `src/sass/icon-font/_mixins.scss` | See full API below. |
| Example usage | `src/sass/woocommerce/_buttons.scss` | Adds the `shopping-cart` icon to WooCommerce's "Add to Cart" button (classic `.add_to_cart_button` + WC Blocks' `.wc-block-components-product-button__button`) via `@include cf-icon-button('shopping-cart')` - zero markup/class changes to WooCommerce's own templates. |

### Full Mixin API (`src/sass/icon-font/_mixins.scss`)

- `cf-icon-content($slug)` - function, returns the raw glyph character for a
  slug (rarely needed directly - the mixins below wrap it).
- `cf-icon-before($slug)` - inserts the icon as a bare `::before`
  pseudo-element on the current selector (no layout/flex/gap added).
- `cf-icon-after($slug)` - same, but `::after`. Full before/after pair is
  available, matching the Button block's left/right icon positions.
- `cf-icon-button($slug, $position: left, $gap: 0.5em)` - the full
  "icon + label" treatment: makes the selector `inline-flex` with a `gap`,
  and picks `cf-icon-before`/`cf-icon-after` based on `$position`. This is
  the Sass equivalent of the Button block's
  `cf-has-icon`/`cf-icon-left`/`cf-icon-right` classes.

```scss
.add_to_cart_button {
  @include cf-icon-button('shopping-cart'); // icon left, default gap
}

.some-other-button {
  @include cf-icon-button('arrow-right', right); // icon right
}

// Lower-level, for a bare pseudo-element without the flex/gap wrapper:
.some-other-selector {
  @include cf-icon-before('search');
}
.another-selector {
  @include cf-icon-after('chevron-down');
}
```

This relies on the font already being loaded globally by
`Chairforce\Lucide_Icons` (Surface 1) - these mixins only add
`font-family`/`content`, they don't re-declare `@font-face`.

### Critical: Literal Glyph Characters, Not `\eXXX` Escapes

`$cf-icon-codepoints` in `_variables.scss` stores each icon as a **literal
Unicode character** (e.g. a real `` glyph character pasted into the file),
**not** a backslash-escaped codepoint string like `"\e151"`. This is
deliberate and load-bearing: escape-sequence `content` values are exactly
what cssnano silently stripped during production minification when this
same content previously went through this pipeline (see Surface 1's
"Why the Icon Font CSS Is NOT in the Webpack/Sass Pipeline"). Literal
characters were the original, verified-working fix for that bug, before
Surface 1 was pulled out of the build entirely as a static file instead.
Verified end-to-end: after `npm run build:assets`, the compiled
`build/public.css` still contains the correct raw UTF-8 bytes for each glyph
(e.g. `\xee\x85\x9c` for `shopping-cart`/U+E15C) — confirm this if you ever
touch `_variables.scss`, don't just eyeball the (invisible-looking)
characters in an editor.

**Do not "clean up" `$cf-icon-codepoints` into `\eXXX` escapes.** If you need
to regenerate/verify the map, use a short one-off script (Python/Node) that
writes the file programmatically via `chr(int(hex, 16))` - typing/copy-pasting
these Private-Use-Area characters through chat/tool text can silently drop
them, so always verify the written file's raw bytes (e.g. `xxd`/`hexdump`)
rather than trusting how they render in an editor.

### How to Add an Icon Here

1. Update `CHAIRFORCE_LUCIDE_ICON_OPTIONS` in `lucide-icon-options.js` first.
2. Look up the codepoint in `node_modules/lucide-static/font/codepoints.json`.
3. Add the literal-character entry to `$cf-icon-codepoints` in
   `src/sass/icon-font/_variables.scss` (see caution above - generate via
   script, verify bytes, don't hand-type).
4. `npm run build:assets`, then spot-check `build/public.css` bytes for the
   new glyph if in doubt.

## Enqueue Rule of Thumb

- Needs to render inside block **content** (editor iframe + frontend) →
  `enqueue_block_assets`.
- Editor UI/chrome only (sidebar controls, toolbar) → `enqueue_block_editor_assets`.
- When in doubt for anything icon/content-related, prefer `enqueue_block_assets`
  and guard with `is_admin()` only if you truly need to exclude the frontend.

## Future: WordPress Core `core/icon` Block

WordPress core ships a native `core/icon` block backed by `WP_Icons_Registry`.
As of the WP version this theme currently targets, that registry is **closed**
to third parties (no public `register()`, no filters) — our Lucide icons cannot
be added to it yet. WP 7.1 introduces public `wp_register_icon_collection()` /
`wp_register_icon()` APIs; once core is upgraded, register a `chairforce`
icon collection (reusing the same curated SVGs/content) from
`Chairforce\Lucide_Icons` so the icons also appear in the native Icon block's
picker.

